{
  "cells": [
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "---\n",
        "title: \"Sydney Radar Data\"\n",
        "author: \"Evan Tate Paterson Hughes\"\n",
        "format:\n",
        "  html:\n",
        "    code-fold: false\n",
        "    toc: true\n",
        "    inclue-in-header: header.html\n",
        "    mathjax: \n",
        "      extensions: [\"breqn\", \"bm\", \"ams\"]\n",
        "jupyter: python3\n",
        "bibliography: Bibliography.bib\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.content-visible unless-format=\"pdf\"}\n",
        "\n",
        "[Index](../index.html)\n",
        "\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "# The Sydney Radar Data Set\n",
        "\n",
        "This is a data set... [more information here and plotting here]\n",
        "\n",
        "# Importing the relevant packages and Loading the data\n",
        "\n",
        "Firstly, we load the relevant libraries and import the data."
      ],
      "id": "80b2de17"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "import jax\n",
        "jax.config.update(\"jax_enable_x64\", True)\n",
        "import os\n",
        "import jax.numpy as jnp\n",
        "import jax.random as rand\n",
        "import pandas as pd\n",
        "import jaxidem.idem as idem\n",
        "import jaxidem.utils as utils\n",
        "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
        "\n",
        "radar_df = pd.read_csv('data/radar_df.csv')"
      ],
      "id": "b6f3e685",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "We should put this data into `jax-idem`s `st_data` type;"
      ],
      "id": "713dee22"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| output: false\n",
        "radar_data = utils.pd_to_st(radar_df, 's2', 's1', 'time', 'z') "
      ],
      "id": "281a601a",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Plotting the data\n",
        "\n",
        "Firstly, lets take a look at the data set. I is a collection of 12 images taken at evenly spaced intervals. \n",
        "We can use the method `st_data.show_plot` to uickly make a plot of data like this;"
      ],
      "id": "3941dcbf"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| output: false\n",
        "radar_data.save_plot('figure/Sydney_plot.png')"
      ],
      "id": "bc2b638b",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "::: {#fig-example layout-ncol=1}\n",
        "\n",
        "![Process](figure/Sydney_plot.png)\n",
        "\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## Modelling\n",
        "\n",
        "We will firstly censor the data, so that we can assess the 'blanks' we filled in."
      ],
      "id": "0d121425"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| eval: false\n",
        "# Censor the data!\n",
        "radar_df_censored = radar_df\n",
        "# remove the final time measurements (for forecast testing)\n",
        "radar_df_censored = radar_df_censored[radar_df_censored['time'] != \"2000-11-03 08:45:00\"]\n",
        "# remove the a specific time (for intracast testing)\n",
        "radar_df_censored = radar_df_censored[radar_df_censored['time'] != \"2000-11-03 10:15:00\"]\n",
        "# three randomly chose indices ('dead pixels')\n",
        "import numpy as np\n",
        "np.random.seed(42) # reproducibility (jax.random is used elsewhere)\n",
        "random_indices = np.random.choice(radar_df_censored.index, size=300, replace=False)\n",
        "radar_df_censored = radar_df_censored.drop(random_indices)\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "# no covariates (besides intercept)\n",
        "radar_data = utils.pd_to_st(radar_df_censored, 's2', 's1', 'time', 'z')"
      ],
      "id": "1786ecab",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "We now create an initial model for this data "
      ],
      "id": "a15a3f7b"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| output: false\n",
        "model = idem.init_model(data=radar_data)"
      ],
      "id": "bbef2302",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "This, by default, creates an invariant kernel model with no covariates beside an intercept, with cosine basis function for the process decomposition.\n",
        "We can now get the marginal data likelihood function of this model with the `get_log_like` method;"
      ],
      "id": "109500af"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "log_marginal = model.get_log_like(radar_data, method=\"sqinf\", likelihood='partial')"
      ],
      "id": "2d37885f",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "We can then use this function to do various inference techniques, like direclty maximising it or Bayesian MCMC methods. It is auto-differentiation compatible, so can easily be dropped into packages like `optax` or `blackjax` for these purposes.\n",
        "\n",
        "The function takes, as an input, an object of type `IdemParams`, which is a `NamedTuple` containing the log variances, (transformed) kernel parameters, and the regression coefficients.\n",
        "The `Model` class has a value of these paramters, `Model.params`, and a method is provided to print these parameters in a clear way;"
      ],
      "id": "36bb22af"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| output: true\n",
        "\n",
        "idem.print_params(model.params)"
      ],
      "id": "1e7cb110",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Maximum Likelihood Estimation\n",
        "\n",
        "Once we have this marginal likelihood, there are a few ways to progress.\n",
        "A good start is with a maximum likelihood method.\n",
        "Obviously, we can no just take this lgo marginal function and maximise it in any way we see fit, but `jaxidem.Model` has a built-in method for this, `Model.fit_mle`. Given data, this will use a method from 'optax' to create a new output model with the fitted parameters."
      ],
      "id": "ea1e860e"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| eval: false\n",
        "\n",
        "import optax\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "fit_model_mle, mle_params = model.fit_mle(radar_data,\n",
        "                                          optimizer = optax.adam(1e-2),\n",
        "                                          max_its = 100,\n",
        "                                          method = 'sqinf')"
      ],
      "id": "a41c4ff6",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| eval: true\n",
        "#| echo: false\n",
        "#| output: false\n",
        "\n",
        "import pickle\n",
        "with open('./pickles/Hamilton/24_4_25/mle_params.pkl', 'rb') as file: \n",
        "    mle_params = pickle.load(file)\n",
        "fit_model_mle = model.update(mle_params)"
      ],
      "id": "97d050fd",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "The resulting parameters are then"
      ],
      "id": "bf456cf4"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "idem.print_params(mle_params)"
      ],
      "id": "4f2f6419",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Of course, we can use any other method to maximise this, by using whatever method desired on the function returned from `Model.get_log_liklehood`.\n",
        "We can update the model with new parameters using the method `Model.update`, and `utils.flatten_and_unflatten` (see documentation) allows working with flat arrays instead of PyTrees if needed.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Posterior Sampling\n",
        "\n",
        "It is obviously desirable to use MCMC methods in order to sample from the posterior distribution.\n",
        "This can be done manually using the log likelihood, or by using the method `Model.sample_posterior`. \n",
        "We need to provide it with a sampling kernel; that is, a method to get from one state to the next.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "## Using Blackjax\n",
        "\n",
        "From there, it is easy to sample from the posterior"
      ],
      "id": "18208127"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| eval: false\n",
        "key = jax.random.PRNGKey(1) # PRNG key\n",
        "inverse_mass_matrix = jnp.ones(model.nparams)\n",
        "num_integration_steps = 10\n",
        "step_size = 1e-5\n",
        "sample, _ = model.sample_posterior(key,\n",
        "                                   n=10,\n",
        "                                   burnin=0,\n",
        "                                   obs_data=radar_data,\n",
        "                                   X_obs=[X_obs for _ in range(T)],\n",
        "                                   inverse_mass_matrix=inverse_mass_matrix,\n",
        "                                   num_integration_steps=num_integration_steps,\n",
        "                                   step_size = step_size,\n",
        "                                   likelihood_method=\"sqinf\",)"
      ],
      "id": "57d53d4f",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| eval: true\n",
        "#| echo: false\n",
        "#| output: false\n",
        "\n",
        "import numpy as np\n",
        "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
        "\n",
        "csv_data = np.loadtxt('data/Chains/HMC_chain_32.csv', delimiter=',')\n",
        "# Convert the NumPy array to a JAX array\n",
        "sample = jnp.array(csv_data)[10000:60000,1:]"
      ],
      "id": "a94fe043",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| output: false\n",
        "num_params = sample.shape[1]\n",
        "fig, axes = plt.subplots(num_params, 1, figsize=(10, 10), sharex=True)\n",
        "for i in range(num_params):\n",
        "    axes[i].plot(sample[:, i], lw=0.8, color='b')\n",
        "    axes[i].set_ylabel('')\n",
        "    axes[i].grid(True)\n",
        "axes[-1].set_xlabel('Iteration')\n",
        "fig.suptitle('Trace Plots', fontsize=16, y=0.95)\n",
        "plt.tight_layout(rect=[0, 0, 1, 0.96]) \n",
        "\n",
        "plt.savefig('figure/hmc_trace.png')"
      ],
      "id": "658b9e07",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "::: {#fig-example layout-ncol=1}\n",
        "\n",
        "![Process](figure/hmc_trace.png)\n",
        "\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "Lets take the posterior mean for some filtering and predicting."
      ],
      "id": "7a1ae38a"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# gets the function used to go from a flat array to a parameter of the type used in the models\n",
        "fparams, unflatten = utils.flatten_and_unflatten(model.params)\n",
        "\n",
        "post_mean = jnp.mean(sample, axis=0)\n",
        "new_params = unflatten(post_mean)\n",
        "\n",
        "new_model = model.update(new_params)\n",
        "\n",
        "filt_data, filt_results = new_model.filter(radar_data, forecast = 3, method=\"kalman\")"
      ],
      "id": "6d553199",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| output: false\n",
        "filt_data.save_plot('figure/filtered_plot.png')"
      ],
      "id": "675a6149",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "::: {#fig-filter layout-ncol=1}\n",
        "\n",
        "![Filtered Process](figure/filtered_plot.png)\n",
        "\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "We've also forecasted the next 3 time points, and we can get them like this"
      ],
      "id": "a8950528"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#nuforecast = filt_results['nuforecast']\n",
        "#Rforecast = filt_results['Rforecast']\n",
        "#\n",
        "#from jax.scipy.linalg import solve_triangular as st\n",
        "\n",
        "#mforecast = st(Rforecast, nuforecast[..., None]).squeeze(-1)\n",
        "\n",
        "mforecast = jnp.vstack((filt_results['ms'], filt_results['mforecast']))\n",
        "\n",
        "# will build this in to the Model.filter method soon!\n",
        "fore_data = idem.basis_params_to_st_data(mforecast, model.process_basis, model.process_grid)"
      ],
      "id": "eb4e3f0a",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| output: false\n",
        "fore_data.save_plot('figure/fore_plot.png')"
      ],
      "id": "570dee9d",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "::: {#fig-fore layout-ncol=1}\n",
        "\n",
        "![Forecasted Process](figure/fore_plot.png)\n",
        "\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "We can also plot the variances computed in the Kalman filter"
      ],
      "id": "d7455e49"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Ps = filt_results['Ps']\n",
        "Pforecast = filt_results['Pforecast']\n",
        "combined = jnp.concatenate([Ps])#, Pforecast])\n",
        "\n",
        "PHI = model.PHI_proc\n",
        "\n",
        "process_variances = PHI @ combined @ PHI.T\n",
        "\n",
        "marginals = jnp.diagonal(process_variances, axis1=1, axis2=2)\n",
        "\n",
        "T = marginals.shape[0]\n",
        "\n",
        "coords = jnp.concatenate([model.process_grid.coords for _ in range(T)])\n",
        "times = jnp.repeat(jnp.arange(T), model.process_grid.coords.shape[0])\n",
        "var_data = utils.st_data(x=coords[:, 0], y=coords[:, 1], times=times, z=marginals.ravel())"
      ],
      "id": "4e801bc4",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| output: false\n",
        "var_data.save_plot('figure/var_plot.png')"
      ],
      "id": "aa310e27",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "::: {#fig-fore layout-ncol=1}\n",
        "\n",
        "![Variances](figure/var_plot.png)\n",
        "\n",
        ":::"
      ],
      "id": "28cbdbf8"
    }
  ],
